#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205133 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how Newton’s method can be used to find local minima or maxima of a smooth function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by approximating the function near the current guess with a parabola.\n\nRecall that in one variable, Newton’s update rule is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the first derivative, and $f''(x)$ is the second derivative.\n\nTo generalize this idea to higher dimensions, we need analogues of the first and second derivatives.\n\n Recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x})$ generalizes the first derivative to multivariable functions, and the Hessian matrix $H_f(\\mathbf{x})$ generalizes the second derivative.\n\nLike before, we aim to find critical points, which are the points where the gradient vanishes:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo once again, optimization becomes a root-finding problem, but now for the gradient vector $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}).$\n\nJust like in one dimension, we approximate $f(\\mathbf{x})$ near our current guess $\\mathbf{x}_n$ using a second-order Taylor expansion\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)^T (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n) + \\frac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n)^T H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nThis is the multivariable analogue of a parabola, a *quadratic surface*.\n\nTo find the minimum of this quadratic surface, we compute its gradient with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ and set it equal to zero. The gradient of the approximation is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nWe then set the gradient equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-∇f({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}) \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}.$ So, we write\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n).}\n$$\n\nNewton’s method can be extremely fast, but only under the following conditions:\n\n- The function $f$ is twice continuously differentiable (i.e., smooth).\n\n- The starting point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is close to a critical point.\n\n- The Hessian $H_f(\\mathbf{x})$ is invertible and preferably positive definite near the solution.\n\nWhen these conditions are met, Newton’s method often converges in just a few iterations." :tutorial/id #uuid "81a83620-3aeb-50ae-93f0-c72ca9482763" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10739 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205158 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard Newton’s method update for multivariable optimization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn higher dimensions, it’s often more useful to avoid calculating the inverse of a large Hessian matrix by rewriting the update as a linear system instead.\n\nLet’s see how.\n\nWe start with the original update formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $\\mathbf{x}_n$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n = -[H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides by $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n){:}$\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nThis is a linear system of equations. It’s mathematically equivalent to the original update rule, but much better for actual computation, since we no longer need to invert the Hessian; we just need to solve for $(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d1525f2-bf49-5f3e-859b-4aba4104f35c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10940 :tutorial/title "Alternative to Newton's Method for Multivariable Functions Using Linear Systems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205203 :tutorial/content "Recall the example we worked on, where we computed one iteration of the Newton–Raphson method. \n\nWe aim to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) =x^2y-2xy^2+x^3-4x,\n$$\n\nin the region $[0,2]\\times[0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$.\n\nNow, let’s carry out one iteration of Newton–Raphson using linear systems.\n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+6(1) & 2(1)-4(1) \\\\ 2(1)-4(1) & -4(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(1)-2(1)^{2}+3(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)^{2}-4(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-1/18 \\\\ -13/18\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the same result we obtained previously." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb0b2e3d-6c4d-5e04-acc7-2f23e216e3e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13152 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205204 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times[0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =x^2y-2xy^2+x^3-4x.\n$$\n\nLet’s calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson method, starting at the point $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+6(1) & 2(1)-4(1) \\\\ 2(1)-4(1) & -4(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(1)-2(1)^{2}+3(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)^{2}-4(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(8)\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{-36}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42a507e6-8e0d-5b36-a901-6612002dd2b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13153 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
